From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:22:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] dndcp: native Wayland clipboard for text, images and RTF

Copy and paste between host and guest does nothing in a Wayland session. The
plugin drives the X11 selection directly, and an Xwayland client cannot reach
native Wayland applications that way.

Add a Wayland backend, chosen at runtime when WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set, that
owns the selection itself through ext-data-control-v1.

Shelling out to wl-clipboard was the obvious approach and it is not
sufficient. wl-copy takes a single --type and serves one payload, so a
selection cannot carry rich text and a plain rendering at the same time. The
host sends both for a rich text copy, and the X11 backend passes both on as
separate selection targets; with wl-copy, either the formatting is dropped or
a plain text target receives the RTF markup. Owning the selection means each
request is answered with the bytes for the type that was asked for, which is
what the X11 backend has always done.

wl-clipboard is still used when the compositor does not implement
ext-data-control-v1, which is the case on GNOME. That path keeps the single
payload limitation and so does not carry RTF.

Reads and writes on the selection are bounded by a deadline rather than left
to block. A peer that stops reading must not wedge the main loop, which also
carries the RPC channel.

The cached copy of what was last sent is updated only after the send
succeeds. Recording it earlier would make a failed send look unchanged on the
next attempt, and that content could then never be copied again.

Tested on Fedora 44 with KDE Plasma 6.7.5 on Wayland, against VMware
Workstation Pro 26H1u1 (26.0.1.25688693) on Windows 10 Pro 22H2.
